-- FINDING 3 (loud for the name, silent for the effect).  Valid time has no reader of its own.  It can be
-- observed only through the fallback of `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`, or through the ledger (RECALL).  So the two
-- days of this case, the conduct day D and the reading day R, can both be read inside a rule only if D rides on
-- the rule-effective axis (directly or by fallback) and R on system time.  The "reverse" mapping (rule-effective
-- time = R, valid time = D) leaves D unreachable.
--
-- Printed: line 1 gives 1 Aug 2012 (R) in both nestings; D (1 Jun 2011) is not observable anywhere.
-- `VALID TIME` is not an identifier (see f03b).
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

`the rule-effective day` MEANS `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`

#EVAL `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 6 2011) (`EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (Date 1 8 2012) `the rule-effective day`)
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (Date 1 8 2012) (`EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 6 2011) `the rule-effective day`)
